Subject: admit the BC-250 board in GPU discovery

Two defaults in upstream discovery drop this board, and both are about integrated
GPUs on a machine that has a discrete one as well. The BC-250 has one GPU, it is
the board's own, and it reports itself integrated.

1. gfx1013 is absent from the list of AMD targets admitted by default. The list
   exists for exactly this case and names one part. Measured on gfx1013 silicon:
   the omission is a missing entry, not a hardware limit.
2. A device the list does not name falls through to the per-library default,
   which admits CUDA, admits a named ROCm target, and drops everything else. A
   Vulkan device on this board has no gfx target to match on, so it is dropped
   and the processor backend is all that is left.

The second change keeps OLLAMA_IGPU_ENABLE=0 working: the branch above returns
early whenever the user set the variable either way.

--- a/discover/runner.go
+++ b/discover/runner.go
@@ -30,6 +30,8 @@
 var defaultIntegratedROCmGFXTargets = map[string]struct{}{
 	// AMD Radeon 8060S / Ryzen AI Max+ 395.
 	"gfx1151": {},
+	// ASRock BC-250, gfx1013, behind the bc250 WDDM display driver.
+	"gfx1013": {},
 }

 func GPUDevices(ctx context.Context, runners []ml.FilteredRunnerDiscovery) []ml.DeviceInfo {
@@ -420,7 +422,10 @@
 	if enabledWithTrueDefault == enabledWithFalseDefault {
 		return enabledWithTrueDefault, true
 	}
-	return false, false
+	// This build targets a board whose only GPU reports itself integrated.
+	// Admit integrated GPUs unless the user set OLLAMA_IGPU_ENABLE, which the
+	// branch above answers.
+	return true, true
 }

 func integratedGPUAllowedByDefault(device ml.DeviceInfo) bool {
